L364: ## Appendix A Other PoS Phenomena Considered
L365: 
L366: We considered but ultimately excluded the following PoS-related phenomena:
L367: 
L368:   * •
L369: Anaphoric one.
L370: Although anaphoric one has been widely studied (cite135†Floor and Akhtar, 2006 ; cite273†Pearl and Mis, 2011 ; cite85†Foraker et al., 2009 , e.g.,), recent empirical work suggests that it may not be strictly constrained by the syntactic representation of its antecedent (cite274†Payne et al., 2013 ), making it an unstable test case for PoS arguments.^{9}^{9} 9 In a pilot study, we attempted to replicate native speaker judgments for anaphoric one but observed inconsistency, aligning with the findings of cite274†Payne et al.
L371: (2013) . Given this inconsistency in adult judgments, it is unclear whether anaphoric one constitutes a viable test case for PoS arguments.
L372:   * •
L373: 
L374: Control and Raising. Control and raising constructions are typically acquired after age 5 (cite275†Chomsky, 1969 ; cite276†Choe and Deen, 2016 ), falling outside the 3–5 year developmental window targeted in this study.
L375: 
L376:   * •
L377: 
L378: Binding Principle C. Although young children show early sensitivity to Binding Principle C (cite103†Crain and McKee, 1985 ), testing this phenomenon requires cross-sentential reference interpretation, introducing discourse-level confounds that are difficult to control.
L379: More broadly, PoS arguments have also been advanced beyond syntax, including in phonology (cite277†Wilson, 2006 ), lexical learning (cite278†Braine et al., 1990 ; cite279†Scott and Fisher, 2009 ), morphology (cite280†Gordon, 1985 ), and semantics (cite281†Crain et al., 2000 ; cite282†Falmagne, 2013 ; cite283†Papafragou and Musolino, 2003 ). We limit our focus on syntactic constructions as they stand at the core of PoS.
L380: ## Appendix B Statistics of the baby and wiki datasets
L381: 
L382: The detailed statistics for our wiki baby-f, and baby datasets can be found in cite284†Table 4 .
L383: ## Appendix C Filtering the Corpus
L384: This section details the procedure used to remove sentences containing direct positive evidence for the target PoS phenomena. Among the five main constructions examined, two (Question Formation and Binding) have identifiable direct positive evidence that could directly support the correct generalization.
L385: Our goal is to construct a conservative filtering procedure that minimizes the risk of leaving potential direct positive evidence in the training corpus, even at the cost of including some false positives.
L386: ##### Parsing and General Strategy
L387: 
L388: All sentences in the baby corpus were parsed using Stanza (cite285†Qi et al., 2020 ). For the filtered condition (baby-f), we applied rule-based heuristics over dependency parses and POS tags. To ensure coverage, rules were deliberately designed to err on the side of over-filtering (i.e., allowing more false positives rather than false negatives).
L389: ##### Question Formation
L390: For question formation, according to cite81†Legate and Yang (2002) ; cite286†Sampson (1989) , the direct positive evidence can be two types of sentences: (i) Is the boy [who is in the corner]_{NP} smiling? and (ii) How could [anyone that was awake]_{NP} not hear that? In other words, the sentences must contain a relative clause modifying the subject. In this case, the children would know that the fronted auxiliary is not the first auxiliary linearly but the first auxiliary hierarchically.
L391: Sentences were removed if they (i) contained a relative clause modifying the subject, and (ii) were interrogative. For (i), we identified sentences containing both acl:relcl and nsubj dependencies where the token labeled nsubj precedes that labeled acl:relcl. For (ii), we detected questions based on the presence of a final question mark, a wh-word, or an auxiliary verb among the first two tokens.
L392: ##### Binding
L393: 
L394: To remove direct positive evidence for reflexive binding, we excluded sentences containing a reflexive ending in self or selves that was preceded by at least two noun or pronoun tokens. POS tags for identifying reflexives and potential antecedents were extracted from Stanza parses.
L395: ##### Sanity Check
L396: 
L397: To validate that our filtering strategy effectively removed direct positive evidence, we performed a manual inspection on a random sample of the filtered dataset (baby-f). We randomly sampled 1,000 sentences containing potential triggers for each phenomenon and examined them individually.
L398: For Question Formation, we retrieved sentences containing a question mark and at least two auxiliaries (11k candidates out of 1.5M). We manually checked for subject-auxiliary inversion where the inverted auxiliary is not the linearly first auxiliary. We found zero examples meeting this condition, confirming that our filtering was successful.
L399: For Binding, we retrieved sentences containing reflexives (5.7k candidates). Potential direct positive evidence consists of sentences where the correct antecedent is distinguishable from distractors only via syntactic structure (c-command) and locality constraints, rather than linear locality.
L400: In our 1k sample, we found only 6 examples constituting such “leakage.” In the list below, we bold the correct antecedents and mark potential distractors in gray to illustrate why these serve as disambiguating evidence:
L401:   * •
L402: 
L403: Evidence against Linear Adjacency: In these cases, the correct antecedent is structural, while the linearly closest noun acts as a distractor (often due to feature mismatch or structural depth).
L404: 
L405:     * –
L406: 
L407: People like her don’t often get themselves murdered.
L408: (Distractor ‘her’ is linearly closest but does not c-command.)
L409: 
L410:     * –
L411: As the burgher ceased, his fellow townsmen wept aloud, and many, amid tears, threw themselves at his feet…
L412: (Distractor ‘tears’ is linearly closest but is an object of a preposition.)
L413: 
L414:     * –
L415: 
L416: All we know is, that master and slave trusted themselves alone to a small bark…
L417: (The model must bind to the coordinate phrase rather than the closest singular noun ‘slave’.)
L418: 
L419:   * •
L420: Evidence for Locality (Binding Domain): In these relative clause structures, the matrix subject c-commands the reflexive but is ruled out by the locality constraint (Principle A). The model receives evidence that c-command alone is insufficient without domain restrictions.
L421: 
L422:     * –
L423: 
L424: Mrs. Cochran swayed against David, who pulled himself together.
L425: (Distractor ‘Mrs. Cochran’ c-commands the reflexive but is outside the local CP; the model must strictly bind within the relative clause.)
L426: 
L427:     * –
L428: I’ll tell the entire story to those folks who think themselves so fine.
L429: (Distractor ‘I’ c-commands but is non-local; correct binding is to the local head ‘folks’.)
L430: 
L431:     * –
L432: 
L433: The boys were not allowed… except three or four who … thought themselves richly paid…
L434: (Distractor ‘The boys’ is the matrix subject, ruled out by locality.)
L435: We take a leakage rate of 30 sentences in 1.5M to be negligible. Additionally, this minimal signal is further compromised by the presence of exempt anaphors and logophors (e.g., reflexives in locative PPs) identified in our sample. For example:
L436: 
L437:   * •
L438: 
L439: I think maybe when people own things and then they pass away, a part of themselves gets printed on those things… (Reflexive in a logophoric context; no c-commanding antecedent.)
L440: 
L441:   * •
L442: Most animals, instead of building a nest in front of themselves, build it round themselves. (Reflexive in a locative PP adjunct; the antecedent is not c-commanding the anaphor and thus exempt from strict binding.)
L443: 
L444: These instances act as confounding noise, providing valid linguistic counter-evidence to strict c-command constraints. Consequently, they counteract the leaked positive evidence, ensuring that the leakage remains too sparse and inconsistent to support robust structural generalization.
