L513: ## Appendix G Benchmark design considerations
L514: 
L515: ##### Yes-no question
L516: 
L517: In PoSH-Bench, we standardize evaluation using minimal pairs across three subordinate clause types tested in previous acquisition studies (cite96†Crain and Nakayama, 1987 ): subject, object, and reduced relative clauses.
L518: 
L519: ##### Islands
L520: 
L521: In PoSH-Bench, we include three subtypes, Complex NP, Wh-, and Adjunct Islands, all drawn from the acquisition literature.
L522: ##### Binding
L523: 
L524: In PoSH-Bench, we therefore include two subtypes, locality and c-command, to assess sensitivity to structural binding constraints
L525: ##### Wanan
L526: 
L527: In our minimal-pair design, we do not contrast grammatical want to forms with ungrammatical wanna forms (e.g., Who do you want to/*wanna go?), because want to is substantially more frequent than wanna in all training corpora, introducing a frequency confound unrelated to structural generalization. Instead, we test whether models learn the structural restriction that wanna may only precede intransitive verbs or transitive verb phrases (see Example cite107†1 ).
L528: ## Appendix H Bayesian mixed model results
L529: 
L530: The Baysian mixed model results are reported in cite151†Table 12 .

